      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Founded near the confluence of the Yuba and Feather Rivers, the town grew to serve gold miners heading to California's gold fields.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuba_City%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Mexican government granted the land that became Yuba City to John Sutter, whose property later yielded the 1848 gold discovery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuba_City%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Yuba City is the principal city of a metropolitan area that includes all of Sutter and Yuba counties, making it California's 21st largest metro.",
